{"key":"C04-01","report_id":"R0068","metric":"latency improvement vs competitive baselines","status":"UNVERIFIED","value":null,"units":null,"baseline":null,"denominator":null,"workload":null,"conditions":null,"hardware_status":"UNVERIFIED","sources":[{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":11,"section":"VI. Simulation Results and Analysis - B.2) Effect of Bandwidths","locator":"Section VI-B.2, para. 1","excerpt":"In contrast, traditional MARL algorithms (MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC) consistently demonstrate higher latency and power usage, underscoring the practical benefits of meta-learning techniques.","supports":["inspection"]},{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":13,"section":"VII. Conclusion","locator":"Section VII, para. 1","excerpt":"Our approach outperformed traditional multi-agent DRL-based algorithms, including MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC, in terms of bandwidth adaptation, UAV scalability, and different memory resources.","supports":["inspection"]}],"reason":"The around 29% latency improvement appears only in the Abstract (p. 1) and is not printed in Methods (Section V, pp. 6-10), Simulation Results and Analysis (Section VI, pp. 10-13, Figs. 4-9), or Conclusion (Section VII, p. 13)."}
{"key":"C04-02","report_id":"R0068","metric":"transmission power improvement vs competitive baselines","status":"UNVERIFIED","value":null,"units":null,"baseline":null,"denominator":null,"workload":null,"conditions":null,"hardware_status":"UNVERIFIED","sources":[{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":11,"section":"VI. Simulation Results and Analysis - B.2) Effect of Bandwidths","locator":"Section VI-B.2, para. 1","excerpt":"In contrast, traditional MARL algorithms (MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC) consistently demonstrate higher latency and power usage, underscoring the practical benefits of meta-learning techniques.","supports":["inspection"]},{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":13,"section":"VII. Conclusion","locator":"Section VII, para. 1","excerpt":"Our approach outperformed traditional multi-agent DRL-based algorithms, including MARL-AC, MARL-DQN, and MARL-SAC, in terms of bandwidth adaptation, UAV scalability, and different memory resources.","supports":["inspection"]}],"reason":"The around 23% transmission power improvement appears only in the Abstract (p. 1) and is not printed in Methods (Section V, pp. 6-10), Simulation Results and Analysis (Section VI, pp. 10-13, Figs. 4-9), or Conclusion (Section VII, p. 13)."}
{"key":"C04-03","report_id":"R0068","metric":"latency gap vs LDTP","status":"UNVERIFIED","value":null,"units":null,"baseline":null,"denominator":null,"workload":null,"conditions":null,"hardware_status":"UNVERIFIED","sources":[{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":11,"section":"VI. Simulation Results and Analysis - B.2) Effect of Bandwidths","locator":"Section VI-B.2, para. 1","excerpt":"When comparing the proposed solutions with the competitive algorithms of traditional RL, the LDTP approach establishes a theoretical sub-optimal benchmark due to its optimized nature, and our proposed meta-MARL method closely matches this benchmark, showcasing its capability to quickly adapt to dynamic environmental conditions.","supports":["inspection"]},{"file":"R0068.pdf","page":12,"section":"VI. Simulation Results and Analysis - B.4) Effect of Memory Resources","locator":"Section VI-B.4, para. 1","excerpt":"Notably, the MARL-meta solution consistently delivers latency performance closest to the optimal LDTP solution, outperforming other traditional MARL approaches.","supports":["inspection"]}],"reason":"The around 9% latency gap relative to LDTP appears only in the Abstract (p. 1) and is not printed in Methods (Section V, pp. 6-10), Simulation Results and Analysis (Section VI, pp. 10-13, Figs. 4-9), or Conclusion (Section VII, p. 13)."}
{"key":"C04-04","report_id":"R0054","metric":"online decision runtime (P50/P95)","status":"OBSERVED","value":"30.92/50.56","units":"ms","baseline":"Six-scheme comparison (GAT-HSMAO, No-GAT, Fixed-Heading, CSI-Only, Fixed-RIS, No-RIS)","denominator":"per decision over 10,000 decisions/scheme (60,000 total)","workload":"GAT-HSMAO online decision inference under frozen policies","conditions":"Measured online time reported separately from training under frozen policies; training ran 30 policies as 15 processes/GPU on two RTX6000PRO GPUs in 2.703 h","hardware_status":"NOT_REPORTED","sources":[{"file":"R0054.pdf","page":5,"section":"IV. Numerical Results - Generalization and runtime","locator":"Section IV, Generalization and runtime, para. 1","excerpt":"Panel (f) gives GAT-HSMAO P50/P95 times of 30.92/50.56 ms over 10,000 decisions/scheme (60,000 total).","supports":["value","units","denominator","workload"]},{"file":"R0054.pdf","page":5,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","locator":"Figure 3(f) caption","excerpt":"(f) reports six-scheme P50-P95 time over 10,000 decisions/scheme.","supports":["baseline","denominator"]},{"file":"R0054.pdf","page":4,"section":"III. Proposed Hierarchical Optimization - B. Inner-Layer Resource Optimization","locator":"Section III-B, para. 2","excerpt":"Measured online time is reported separately from training under frozen policies.","supports":["conditions"]},{"file":"R0054.pdf","page":5,"section":"IV. Numerical Results - Generalization and runtime","locator":"Section IV, Generalization and runtime, para. 1","excerpt":"30 policies run as 15 processes/GPU on two RTX6000PRO GPUs in 2.703 h.","supports":["hardware","inspection"]}],"reason":"P50/P95 online decision runtime of 30.92/50.56 ms is printed in Section IV and Figure 3(f); two RTX6000PRO GPUs are reported for training only, while online runtime measurement hardware is NOT_REPORTED."}
{"key":"C04-05","report_id":"R0062","metric":"Stage 1 collaborative inference frame rate","status":"OBSERVED","value":"~ 3","units":"FPS","baseline":"NOT_APPLICABLE","denominator":"frames per second","workload":"Collaborative DNN inference across four UAVs","conditions":"Stage 1: four UAVs (UAV#1 to UAV#4) performing collaborative inference","hardware_status":"REPORTED","sources":[{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":6,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Effect of UAV Unavailability","locator":"Page 6, Effect of UAV Unavailability, para. 1","excerpt":"First, four UAVs (i.e., UAV#1 to UAV#4) were selected to perform inference collaboratively, as Fig. 6 illustrated, the CoEl system can process the video stream at a speed of ~ 3 FPS.","supports":["value","units","workload","conditions"]},{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":6,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Effect of UAV Unavailability","locator":"Figure 6, Stage 1 label","excerpt":"Collaborative inference with four UAVs","supports":["conditions"]},{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":5,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Experiment Setup","locator":"Page 5, Experiment Setup, para. 1","excerpt":"This prototype system contains four UAVs, an NVIDIA Jetson Nano developer kit is set on UAV#1 as computing unit, and an NVIDIA Jetson TX2 as well as two Jetson Nanos are deployed on other three UAVs respectively.","supports":["hardware"]}],"reason":"Stage 1 four-UAV collaborative inference frame rate is printed as ~ 3 FPS; baseline is NOT_APPLICABLE because Stage 1 is the initial four-UAV configuration."}
{"key":"C04-06","report_id":"R0062","metric":"Stage 2 collaborative inference frame rate with one UAV unavailable","status":"OBSERVED","value":"2","units":"FPS","baseline":"Stage 1 four-UAV collaborative inference (~ 3 FPS)","denominator":"frames per second","workload":"Collaborative DNN inference with one UAV unavailable","conditions":"Stage 2: communication program of UAV#3 disabled (one UAV unavailable); eCoEI updates partition strategy among remaining UAVs","hardware_status":"REPORTED","sources":[{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":6,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Effect of UAV Unavailability","locator":"Page 6, Effect of UAV Unavailability, para. 1","excerpt":"The inference process was not interrupted, but at a lower speed, 2 FPS, in the experiment.","supports":["value","units"]},{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":6,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Effect of UAV Unavailability","locator":"Figure 6, Stage 2 label","excerpt":"A UAV is unavailable","supports":["conditions"]},{"file":"R0062.pdf","page":5,"section":"Performance Evaluation - Experiment Setup","locator":"Page 5, Experiment Setup, para. 1","excerpt":"This prototype system contains four UAVs, an NVIDIA Jetson Nano developer kit is set on UAV#1 as computing unit, and an NVIDIA Jetson TX2 as well as two Jetson Nanos are deployed on other three UAVs respectively.","supports":["hardware"]}],"reason":"Stage 2 frame rate when one UAV (UAV#3) is unavailable is printed as 2 FPS (no approximation marker printed in the Stage 2 sentence)."}
